@* Batch M-D4 ("the recursive reader"): the ONE in-text mention-scan-and-
   render primitive -- before this batch, Reader.razor's own verse text and
   MiniReaderExpand.razor's own verse text each ran an IDENTICAL
   `@foreach (var seg in PlaceMentions.Scan(...))` loop with near-identical
   markup (differing only in CSS class/testid prefixes and each file's own
   copy of the `Blink`/mention-open one-liners) -- two hand-copies of the
   exact same rendering, the "forbidden parallel-path disease" the batch
   brief names, one layer further in than the verse LINE itself. This
   component is that loop, extracted once: given a verse's text plus its own
   attested places/persons (`GET /api/chapter/{cref}`'s per-verse `places`/
   `persons` arrays, `VerseOut`), it renders the SAME plain-prose/place-span/
   person-span sequence `Explore/PlaceMentions.cs`'s `PlaceMentions.Scan`
   already computes -- see that file's own doc comment for the full
   scan/overlap/Sin-guard rule (case-sensitive, whole-word, longest-match-
   wins, Place-before-Person tie-break); nothing about THAT rule changes
   here, only where the rendering loop over its result lives.

   VerseLine.razor (the canonical verse-ROW renderer) uses this for its own
   verse-text span; VerseTextSection.razor's own one-verse/passage FOCUS
   preview (previously bare, unscanned text -- decision 3, "name links...
   render wherever verse text renders") now ALSO uses this directly, with no
   verse-num/xref-marker/row wrapper of its own to bring along -- this
   component renders ONLY the text-and-mentions sequence, never a wrapping
   element OF THE WHOLE (Batch RED-1 below is the one narrow exception, over
   a red-letter SUB-RANGE only, never the whole output), so either caller
   supplies whatever container (a `<span>` inside a clickable row, or a bare
   `<p>`) fits its own context.

   ClassPrefix doubles as BOTH the CSS class root and the data-testid root
   (true in both pre-existing implementations: "verse-mention"/"verse-
   mention-person" restyled onto ONE token, "popover-reader-mention"/
   "popover-reader-mention-person" the same way) -- callers keep their own
   PRE-EXISTING prefix (testids follow existing conventions; house pattern)
   so no existing Playwright locator changes: Reader.razor passes
   "verse-mention", MiniReaderExpand.razor passes "popover-reader-mention"
   (plus its own established TestIdSuffix, unchanged), VerseTextSection's
   new compact-view usage passes the new "popover-verse-mention" (its own
   distinct identity -- a compact FOCUS preview is not a mini-reader row,
   see that file's own comment -- reusing `.popover-reader-mention`'s exact
   CSS via a compound selector in app.css, not a re-derived rule).

   M-D4 fix round 1 (R-M1, review Important-1): PassageList.razor's own
   compact preview text -- cross-refs, THE SCRIPTURES, place est/dest,
   event witnesses, every consumer of that shared component -- was the one
   remaining bare/unscanned rendering of verse text left client-wide once
   this batch's own unification otherwise finished; it now calls this
   component too, passing "popover-passage-mention" plus its own
   pre-existing per-entry TestIdSuffix (the SAME disambiguation
   MiniReaderExpand passes one level up, reused rather than re-derived).

   Blink (BLINK-1) is consolidated here too -- both pre-existing copies were
   the identical one-liner (`MapInterop.BlinkPlace(JS, placeId, active)`),
   kept as two copies only because neither file wanted a THIRD shared file
   for one line each (Reader.razor's own prior comment, verbatim) -- now
   moot, since this component already exists for the mention markup itself.

   BATCH RED-1 ("Red letters on Jesus' words in every translation," owner
   order 2026-08-25): the actual place/person scan-and-render loop moved to
   MentionScan.razor (see that file's own doc comment) so THIS component
   could wrap a red-letter sub-RANGE in a `.words-of-christ` span without
   duplicating that markup. `ComputeRuns` below splits `Text` at the
   (already sorted, non-overlapping -- `red_letter_spans.rs`'s own
   alignment law) `WordsOfChrist` char-offset boundaries into an ordered
   list of RUNS, each either red or not; each run is scanned for mentions
   INDEPENDENTLY (`MentionScan` over just that run's own substring) and, if
   red, wrapped in one `.words-of-christ` span. The overwhelmingly common
   case (`WordsOfChrist` empty -- most verses, and every non-KJV
   corpus/edition) returns exactly ONE non-red run covering the whole text,
   so NO extra wrapper element is ever added for a verse that carries no
   red-letter span at all -- byte-identical rendering to before this batch.
   A mention name that happens to straddle a red/narration boundary (never
   observed in the real committed data) would not be detected as one whole
   span in the affected half -- a disclosed, accepted approximation, the
   same class `PlaceMentions.cs`'s own header comment already establishes
   for pronouns/en-dashes. *@
@using BibleAtlas.Client.Explore

@foreach (var run in ComputeRuns(Text, WordsOfChrist))
{
    @if (run.IsRed)
    {
        <span class="words-of-christ">
            <MentionScan Text="@run.Text" Places="@Places" Persons="@Persons" Verse="@Verse" ClassPrefix="@ClassPrefix" TestIdSuffix="@TestIdSuffix" OnExplore="OnExplore" />
        </span>
    }
    else
    {
        <MentionScan Text="@run.Text" Places="@Places" Persons="@Persons" Verse="@Verse" ClassPrefix="@ClassPrefix" TestIdSuffix="@TestIdSuffix" OnExplore="OnExplore" />
    }
}

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Text { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public IReadOnlyList<PlaceRefDto> Places { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public IReadOnlyList<PersonRefDto> Persons { get; set; } = default!;

    // Batch RED-1: optional (default empty) so every PRE-EXISTING caller
    // this batch does not touch (none, as of this batch -- every real
    // caller is updated in the same commit -- but a future one) still
    // compiles and renders exactly as before: zero spans means
    // ComputeRuns returns one whole-text, non-red run, identical to this
    // component's own pre-RED-1 behavior.
    [Parameter] public IReadOnlyList<WordsOfChristSpanDto> WordsOfChrist { get; set; } = Array.Empty<WordsOfChristSpanDto>();

    // The verse number this text belongs to -- testid interpolation only
    // (`{ClassPrefix}-{Verse}-{id}`); this component itself is otherwise
    // verse-number-agnostic (VerseTextSection's own multi-verse passage
    // compact view calls this once PER VERSE in a loop, each with ITS OWN
    // Verse -- see that file's own comment).
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public int Verse { get; set; }

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string ClassPrefix { get; set; } = default!;

    // MiniReaderExpand's own established multi-instance disambiguation --
    // see MentionText.razor's own comment on this identical parameter.
    [Parameter] public string TestIdSuffix { get; set; } = "";

    // Threaded straight to whichever popover-stack/active-node mechanism
    // the caller owns (ctx.PushAsync inside a popover; Reader.razor's own
    // OnLineExplore at the top level) -- this component never decides HOW a
    // node opens, only WHICH node a mention click means, same "a
    // caller/provider supplies the real push, this component only ever
    // proposes a node" contract every other explorable row in this popover
    // platform already follows (MiniReaderExpand.razor's own prior doc
    // comment on OnExplore, verbatim precedent).
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public EventCallback<IExplorable> OnExplore { get; set; }

    /// Batch RED-1: one contiguous slice of `text`, either wholly INSIDE a
    /// red-letter span or wholly outside every one -- module doc comment
    /// has the full "why" and the one disclosed approximation.
    public readonly record struct TextRun(string Text, bool IsRed);

    /// Pure; covered end-to-end by tests/ux/reader-red-letters.spec.ts
    /// (no client unit-test project exists). `spans` is
    /// trusted sorted/non-overlapping (the server's own alignment walk
    /// guarantees this -- `red_letter.rs`'s `align_verse`: "a cursor that
    /// advances past each successful match"), but this function still
    /// defends against a malformed/out-of-range span (a clamp, never a
    /// throw) rather than trusting the wire blindly.
    public static List<TextRun> ComputeRuns(string text, IReadOnlyList<WordsOfChristSpanDto> spans)
    {
        if (spans.Count == 0)
        {
            return new List<TextRun> { new(text, false) };
        }
        var runs = new List<TextRun>();
        var cursor = 0;
        foreach (var s in spans)
        {
            var start = Math.Clamp(s.Start, 0, text.Length);
            var end = Math.Clamp(s.End, start, text.Length);
            if (start > cursor)
            {
                runs.Add(new TextRun(text[cursor..start], false));
            }
            if (end > start)
            {
                runs.Add(new TextRun(text[start..end], true));
            }
            cursor = Math.Max(cursor, end);
        }
        if (cursor < text.Length || runs.Count == 0)
        {
            runs.Add(new TextRun(text[cursor..], false));
        }
        return runs;
    }
}
